Z||000011||DJRice||08-22-2000||11:12 AM||||If you are interested in looking at the "microwave type" switches, they are called membrane switches and you can find some at  <A HREF="http://www.digikey.com" TARGET=_blank>www.digikey.com</A> <P>Another decent source for electronic goodies is  <A HREF="http://www.jameco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.jameco.com</A> <P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||08-22-2000||02:04 PM||||I've been watching this thread..... You've overlooked something...<P>The Power Window circuit can draw up to 30 amps before tripping it's breaker. (It's one of the 30 amp cans in the fuse block. It's a thermal breaker, not a fuse.)<P>Your switches have to be able to handle at least as much current as the motors actually draw which is likely 10-15 amps under normal condistions. There are no relays in this circuit. The switches take full motor load.<P>This means you can't use membrane or most other small switches. They will likely burn out in short order if not immediatly unless you have to add a relay setup. <P>You will need 2 relays per motor. The exact relays depend on how many switches are involved.<P>Finding a single switch that is mechanically like the existing switch should be fun. The wiring for that type switch and a relay is pretty easy since only one set of contacts can be closed.<P>It may seem easier to use 2 switches but if they are both pressed at the same time you'll likely short out the system. The wiring for safely using 2 switches should get really interesting.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||08-22-2000||04:24 PM||||OK.  No micro switches for the windows.  Hmmm...Other suggestions??  Like I said, I'm going for stealth.  I had thought about making the p/mirror switch into a wired (but removable) remote.  Since I don't move my mirrors much anyway, I could leave it unplugged and put away until I need it.  Unfortunately, I can't do the same with the p/window switches.  I had thought about installing them in the doors (both on the drivers side and one on the pass), but it may be a while before I get around to doing anything custom to the doors.<P>Ogre--The micro switches (or maybe membrane switches) <I>will</I> work fine for the headlights, marker lights, trunk release, etc, right???||||reg||1
Z||000014||VISCERAL||08-22-2000||05:10 PM||||Bill Osmer, please tell us more about your 98 grand prix switches. Any pics would be greatly appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Taijiguy||08-22-2000||08:13 PM||||I don't think you'll see me do this much, but I disagree with Ogre on the window switch thing. While the windows may draw 30 amps (but I seriously doubt it) most switches like the ones you're going to be looking at are going to be rated at x amps at 120 (or even 600) volts. that same number of amps at 12 volts isn't going to generate the same amount of heat (if I recall voltage X current (in amps) = power(wattage)(which is the heat generated)) with the idea I had of the touchpad from a microwave, you would need to run relays for some circuits (including the windows) and others could be handled by solid state (transistor) circuits, since it would only have momentary touch sensitive contact switches. And the wiring for using two switches on the power windows would be simple actually, it would be a matter of integrating the two relays so that they depend on each other for power, so that if one is activated, no power is available to the second. Piece of cake. Don't be discouraged. <BR>I AM interested in the conversion to the backlit switches though, whoever had that idea?<P>-Taiji||||reg||1
