Z||000021||lawrence||08-22-2000||10:33 PM||||Actually, 12 volts DC is much harder on switches than AC, just about at any current.  Since AC goes through 0 volts, it's easier on the contacts when breaking the circuit.  If you've read any of my articles, you will note that I specify getting DC rated relays when doing work with car circuits.<P>As for membrane switches, they will probably fry the instant you use them for the windows, lights, trunk or probably anything else in the car.  They are generally rated in the 100 milliamp range or less.  You might not even be able to drive the large relays with these tiny switches.<P>If you really want to use a membrane or keyboard switch, you should use a keyboard encoder chip like a 74C922 (or '923, depending on how many keys you have) and then you need to feed that into a 74C154 to decode the 4 bits into 16 individual outputs.  These outputs will still need a transistor to drive the relays that will do the work.<P>It isn't as hard as it may sound, but it isn't as simple as wiring in new switches.  You will need to make a printed circuit board, and build a regulated power supply for the chips.  The downside is that you will only be able to use one switch at a time, since the decoder will only output the code for one switch at a time.<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000022||Monkeyman||08-22-2000||10:40 PM||||Oh.<P>I think I'll just go with some "conventional" switches.  What should I look for as far as a rating?  And do you have any sources?||||reg||1
Z||000023||theogre||08-22-2000||11:34 PM||||I didn't say the motors draw 30 amps. They likely draw in the 10-15 range. The breaker is sized for both motors to run at the same time.<P>Trust me. I've tried running headlights and other high current car things on switches other than the original. A good one was years ago when I wanted a differant dimmer for the headlights. (The road salt you track into the car can play hell with them.) The poor little switch melted. Burnt the hell out of me when it did. had to go back to the floor switch with it's huge contacts. Someone told me about the same thing as above.... It's only a few VDC, it'll work..... Not. A pair of High beam headlamps draw around 15 amps DC.<P>Switches are funny things. There current rating and voltage rating are not related exactly the way people think. The voltage rating is the voltage that switch will cleanly interupt without frying. <P>The current is the current it will carry on cloesd contacts reguardless of voltage. (but you don't want to put more than the rated voltage thru it.)<P>For most switches the ratings are done based on 60hz AC. If you change from 60hz to some other frequency it usually changes the switch ratings. For example if you move from 60hz to 400hz it changes the behavior of the switch. (400hz is common in military applications.)<P>Larger switches will often have both the 60hzAC and DC current rating printed on them. Small switches just don't have room. The DC rating and the AC rating are rarely the same.<P>Yes, it's bloody confusing at times. To make it worse you have to watch out for inductive DC loads.(motors and such) They can spike back way more than enough to fry hell out of contacts. That's why allot of the car's relays have shunt diodes in them. The shunts kill the feed back from the load before it cooks the contact.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Monkeyman||08-23-2000||06:02 AM||||Ogre--Surely there are other switches besides the stock ones that will work, aren't there?  I can't think of any way to get the stealthy look I want with the stock switches.  Let's see what I've got so far.<P>I can make a removable, wired remote for the p/mirrors (no problem).  I can run the OE switches (not necessarily from a Fiero) for the p/w into the door panels (I'll just have to step up "production" of the doors).  Seems like I can use just about anything for small accessories such as driving lights, neon, etc since those kits generally come with some fairly cheap switches anyway.  That leaves:  headlights/marker lights and trunk release.  What can I come up with that would look good and be out of the way for those?||||reg||1
Z||000025||Taijiguy||08-23-2000||07:31 AM||||Monkey: The trunk release will work on a pretty small switch, it's only a spst momentary contact switch, simple hookup. The headlights can be run off a multi-contact latching relay from a stealth switch (a-la microwave oven touchpad) You could even convert the dash dimmer to digital, and run it from the same type of switches. Granted, if you were to go with the microwave touch pad type setup, you would have to actually run a two stage activation circuit, using a transistor circuit from the touchpad, to activate the relay, since the touchpad switches aren't designed to handle the current of the relay. (btw, I think I paid about $7.00 for the last one I bought, and despite the nay-sayers, I did everything we've been discussing already on another car, without incident) It seems daunting, but it's really very simple, and the cost of components is negligible. Don't be discourgaed, and like I said, I'll help you design the circuits. Don't sweat it.<P>I understand what you're saying Ogre in regards to switch rating. And without changing the Fiero Forum into the "Electronic Geeks Forum" when they rate a switch at 600 volts for example, they still rate the current at that same voltage. There is alot more, some of which you touched on when talking about going from AC to DC voltage, but let's face it, window motors might spike at a few amps, tops. It would have to be a pretty cheap or cheesy switch to not be able to tolerate short spikes in amperage. And even IF the switches he's considering couldn't handle the current ratings alone, it's easy to install relays to perform the function. <P>(Hey, I know what I'm talking about, you're lookin at a guy who did the "440 Shuffle" once in the Navy. And yes, we used 400Hz AC for missle guidance system power- MT-2/SS, USS Andrew Jackson, SSBN 619 Gold crew)<P>-Taiji <p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 08-23-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000026||Bill Osmer||08-23-2000||11:23 AM||||Ogre / Taji / VISCERAL / all -<P>RE: the 98 back-lit Grand Prix switches -<P>For those going to the NIFE Fieroama (Sept 10, in Oakbrook, IL), I'll be there and I can show the installation off.<P>The rest - I'm being encouraged to write up an article, which will, of course, have pictures. I'll post pictures before the article.<P><P>------------------<BR>Bill Osmer<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/forumcardsig2.jpg"> ||||reg||2
